flowchart TB
%% Five independent controls. That they are independent is the point, and it is why they are drawn
%% as five stacked boxes with no arrows between them rather than as a chain: any one of them being
%% wrong has to leave the other four standing. A single gate that "mostly works" is one bug away
%% from nothing; five narrow ones are one bug away from four.

%% Every one of these is a file in git, so widening any of them is a diff somebody reads.

%% The invisible links exist only to force one box per row. They assert nothing.

  rbac["1. RBAC<br/>read-only: no write verb,<br/>no secrets, no exec"]
  netpol["2. Network policy<br/>default-deny both directions"]
  admission["3. Admission<br/>Kyverno on the agent's<br/>own identity"]
  secrets["4. Secrets<br/>one path in, from a vault"]
  prgate["5. The pull request<br/>a human merges everything"]

  rbac ~~~ netpol
  netpol ~~~ admission
  admission ~~~ secrets
  secrets ~~~ prgate

  %% All five are the same blue on purpose. Colouring them differently would imply a hierarchy or
  %% a ranking between controls that does not exist — the argument of this figure is that they are
  %% peers, and the drawing should not argue against it.
  classDef control fill:#121820,stroke:#71b7ff,stroke-width:2px,color:#e7edf3
  class rbac,netpol,admission,secrets,prgate control